      "title": "Ejectives and gemination",
      "language_code": "am",
      "steps": [
        {
          "kind": "learn",
          "duration_seconds": 22,
          "body_en": "Amharic is written in Ge'ez Fidel (assets/data/scripts/tracks_catalog.json, geez_fidel for am). Ejective consonants and geminated (long) consonants are part of spoken Amharic sound patterns called out in ELEVATION §11.2 — this micro-lesson uses only phrases already in assets/data/game_content.json and licensed audio manifest rows, not invented lemmas.",
          "source_ids": [
            "ELEVATION.md#§11.2:L1790",
            "assets/data/scripts/tracks_catalog.json#geez_fidel",
            "assets/data/scripts/fidel_geez_lessons.json",
            "P7-933"
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "see",
          "duration_seconds": 28,
          "body_en": "Hear cited A1/A2 phrases, then replay slow-motion mouth placement when native video arrives (EXT-13). Notice ejective syllables ቀ and ቆ in the story and feelings phrases, and the lengthened ጣ in በጣም inside the very-good phrase.",
